<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 20px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme: Healthy Minds for Healthy Bodies – Lesson # 3</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e0f2f7;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 3</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e0f2f7;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Speaking</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e0f2f7;"><b>Grade:</b> 12th Grade (CEFR: B1.3)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e0f2f7;"><b>Scenario:</b> Checking in on Health</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e0f2f7;"><b>Theme:</b> Healthy Minds for Healthy Bodies</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e0f2f7;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e0f2f7;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e0f2f7;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Speaking: Productive: Describe dreams, hopes, and ambitions. Interactive: Discuss general habits and their impact on life.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Speaking: Can describe personal dreams and ambitions related to health and wellness, using clear and organized speech (e.g., "I hope to become a nutritionist so I can help people make healthier food choices.")<br>Can discuss general health habits and their impact on well-being in a conversation with peers (e.g., "Exercising regularly can improve your mood and help manage stress.")<br>Can express hypothetical ideas about achieving life balance with the help of health technology, using appropriate language structures (e.g., "If I had a sleep tracker, I would try to improve my bedtime routine.")</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center; margin-top: 30px; margin-bottom: 15px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e0f2f7; color: #1a5276; width: 33%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e0f2f7; color: #1a5276; width: 33%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e0f2f7; color: #1a5276; width: 33%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <!-- STAGE 1 -->
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task (Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</b><br>Students engage with the topic, activate prior knowledge, and are introduced to key language. Teacher models target structures and clarifies meaning and pronunciation.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays a series of images related to different health habits (e.g., someone meditating, exercising, drinking water, tracking sleep on an app). Students work in small groups and orally describe what they see, discussing which habits they *maintain* or *might try* to *improve* their *well-being*. They share one idea per group with the class, encouraging simple sentences like "I think regular *exercise* can *boost* your *energy*."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models sentences using the present perfect continuous for ongoing habits and the second conditional for hypothetical improvements. For example: "I <b>have been</b> *practicing* *mindfulness* for a year to *manage* *stress*." and "If I had a *sleep tracker*, I <b>would try</b> to *improve* my *bedtime routine*." The teacher also models the pronunciation of /b/ in "<b>b</b>alance" and "<b>b</b>oost" contrasting with /p/ in "<b>p</b>ractice" and "<b>p</b>revent," and the /s/ in "stress" and "sleep" versus /z/ in "exercise." Emphasis is placed on sentence rhythm for present perfect continuous.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"When I say, 'I <b>have been</b> *exercising* daily,' am I talking about an action I started and finished, or one that is still ongoing?" (Expected answer: "Still ongoing.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I say, 'If I had more free time, I <b>would join</b> a yoga class,' is this a real situation right now, or an imaginary one?" (Expected answer: "An imaginary situation.")</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>

        <!-- STAGE 2 -->
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b><br>New language and content are presented through authentic materials, providing context for the target vocabulary and grammar.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> An audio recording of a short interview dialogue (approx. 2-3 minutes) between a wellness coach and a high school student discussing their *health* *habits*, *dreams*, and the potential impact of *health technology* on *well-being*. The dialogue incorporates phrases with the present perfect continuous, second conditional, and modals of possibility, along with target vocabulary like *nutrition*, *hydration*, *mindfulness*, *stress*, *tracker*, *routine*, *balance*, *self-care*, *preventive*.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> The learner can participate in conversations on familiar topics related to personal health and well-being, exchanging information and opinions, and describing personal aspirations.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks (Listening for gist and specific information, leading to speaking):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students listen to the dialogue once and answer a gist question orally: "What is the main topic of the conversation between the coach and the student?" (Expected answer: "The student's general health habits and future health aspirations.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students listen a second time and work in pairs to verbally identify specific examples of:
                        <ul style="list-style-type: circle; margin-top: 4px;">
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 2px; text-align: justify;">A habit the student *has been maintaining*.</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 2px; text-align: justify;">A hypothetical situation they discuss (e.g., "If I had X, I would Y").</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 2px; text-align: justify;">A health *suggestion* or *recommendation* made by either person.</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">7-10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>

        <!-- STAGE 3 -->
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b><br>Students practice the new language in controlled and semi-controlled activities, focusing on accuracy and proper use of grammatical structures and vocabulary in speaking.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Oral practice of present perfect continuous (ongoing efforts), second conditional (hypothetical well-being), modals for possibility (health suggestions/recommendations). Vocabulary: *health, fitness, well-being, hydration, mindfulness, balance, habits, stress, diet, routine, energy, nutrition, self-care, tracker, app*; verbs: *exercise, drink, sleep, practice, recommend, maintain, improve, track, manage, relax*; adjectives: *balanced, hydrated, healthy, regular, mindful, energized, nutritious, consistent*.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice – Sentence Completion (Oral):</b> The teacher provides sentence starters on the board or projector, and students complete them orally in pairs, ensuring correct grammar and vocabulary.
                        <ul style="list-style-type: circle; margin-top: 4px;">
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 2px; text-align: justify;">"For the past month, I have been..." (e.g., "I <b>have been</b> *drinking* more water for *hydration*.")</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 2px; text-align: justify;">"If I had a personal health coach, I would..." (e.g., "If I had a personal health coach, I <b>would try</b> to *improve* my *nutrition*.")</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 2px; text-align: justify;">"To *manage* *stress*, you *might*..." (e.g., "To *manage* *stress*, you <b>might try</b> *meditation*.")</li>
                        </ul>
                        The teacher monitors and corrects pronunciation, particularly /b/ vs /p/ and /s/ vs /z/, and sentence rhythm.
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-Controlled Practice – "Health Habit Chain":</b> Students sit in a circle. The first student states a health habit they *have been practicing*. The next student repeats the previous habit and adds a *hypothetical improvement* using the second conditional. The third student repeats the first two and adds a *suggestion* using a modal.
                        <ul style="list-style-type: circle; margin-top: 4px;">
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 2px; text-align: justify;">Student A: "I <b>have been</b> *exercising* regularly for my *fitness*."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 2px; text-align: justify;">Student B: "You <b>have been</b> *exercising* regularly. If I had more time, I <b>would join</b> a gym to *boost* my *energy* too."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 2px; text-align: justify;">Student C: "You <b>have been</b> *exercising* regularly, and if you had more time, you <b>would join</b> a gym. You <b>could</b> also *track* your *activity level* with an *app*."</li>
                        </ul>
                        Teacher encourages polite tone and empathy where appropriate.
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction – "Expressing Dreams":</b> Students work in pairs. Partner A describes one dream or ambition related to *health* or *well-being* using the present perfect continuous (e.g., "I <b>have been</b> *planning* my career as a *nutritionist* because I want to help people achieve *balanced* *lifestyles*."). Partner B responds by expressing empathy and then asks a follow-up question using a second conditional or modal, for example, "That sounds tough. If you were a nutritionist, what *might* be your first recommendation for someone with high *stress*?" They swap roles.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-18 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>

        <!-- STAGE 4 -->
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b><br>Students apply the acquired language in a realistic, communicative task, focusing on fluency, interaction, and task completion in a scenario directly related to their context.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Role-play simulation – "Health and Wellness Consultation."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b> Students work in groups of three: two students are peers discussing their *health* *goals* and *challenges*, and the third acts as a 'Peer Wellness Advisor.' The scenario is "Checking in on Health," where one peer expresses a specific health concern or ambition (e.g., "I <b>have been</b> *struggling* with *stress* lately and need to find *balance*," or "My dream is to *improve* my *fitness* to run a marathon, but I don't know where to start."). The other peer listens empathetically and the 'Peer Wellness Advisor' gives *suggestions* and asks *hypothetical questions* to encourage discussion about *habits* and *well-being*.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students must:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use the present perfect continuous to describe ongoing *health efforts* or challenges (e.g., "I <b>have been</b> *trying* to *improve* my *diet*.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use the second conditional to discuss *hypothetical situations* or aspirations (e.g., "If I had a *nutritionist*, I <b>would know</b> exactly what to eat." or "If I *practiced* *meditation* more consistently, I <b>might feel</b> calmer.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Employ modals of possibility to give *health suggestions* or *recommendations* politely (e.g., "You <b>might</b> consider using a *tracker* to *monitor* your *sleep patterns*." or "You <b>could try</b> some *mindfulness* exercises.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Utilize a range of vocabulary words from the *health* and *wellness* theme.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Demonstrate empathy and use a polite, respectful tone.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">The teacher circulates, observing fluency, interaction, and accurate application of target language without interrupting.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>

        <!-- STAGE 5 -->
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b><br>Students receive feedback on their performance, allowing them to reflect on their learning and identify areas for improvement. This stage consolidates understanding and production of the target language.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist for speaking during the role-play, focusing on specific criteria. Peer feedback is also encouraged.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students present their "Health and Wellness Consultation" role-play to another group or the whole class (time permitting). Alternatively, the teacher uses their observation notes from the individual groups.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Speaking):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Intelligible Pronunciation:</b> Clear articulation, especially of /b/, /p/, /s/, /z/ sounds, and natural sentence rhythm in present perfect continuous.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of Target Vocabulary:</b> Appropriate and varied use of *health, well-being, mindfulness, balance, stress, hydration, nutrition, self-care, tracker*, etc.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Grammar Accuracy:</b> Correct application of present perfect continuous, second conditional, and modals for possibility.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Fluency and Coherence:</b> Speaking with reasonable pace, minimal hesitation, and logical flow of ideas.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pragmatic and Sociolinguistic Competence:</b> Giving polite suggestions, expressing empathy, and maintaining a respectful tone.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Task Completion:</b> Successfully addressing the scenario and discussing health goals/challenges.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket (Oral Reflection):</b> Students verbally answer the following:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is one new *health habit* you *might* start or *improve* after today's discussion?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How confident do you feel using the second conditional to discuss *hypothetical* *well-being* improvements?" (Scale of 1-5, or describe.)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>

        <!-- STAGE 6 -->
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b><br>Students and teacher reflect on the learning process. The teacher considers the effectiveness of the lesson and plans for future adjustments and connections to upcoming lessons.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Reflection Prompt (Oral or written on a note card):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was the most challenging part of describing your *dreams* or giving *health suggestions* today, and what *could* you do to *improve* it?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How did using phrases like 'I <b>have been</b>...' or 'If I had..., I <b>would</b>...' help you express your ideas about *health* and *well-being* more clearly?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"The role-play activity (Stage 4) was effective in promoting natural interaction and usage of the target structures, especially the polite suggestions with modals and expressing empathy. Students demonstrated good understanding of the scenario."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Some students still showed hesitation with the present perfect continuous sentence rhythm; more explicit modeling and choral repetition might be needed in future warm-ups. A visual aid with common collocations for health habits could also strengthen vocabulary recall during spontaneous speaking."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Adjustments needed: Ensure clearer prompts for the 'Health Habit Chain' to guide grammar use more explicitly for those who struggled."</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to Next Lesson (Lesson 4 – Writing Composition):</b> In the next lesson, students will take their ideas and discussions about *healthy habits*, *dreams*, and *hypothetical health improvements* from today's speaking activities and use them to write a short reflective essay or a persuasive paragraph about maintaining a *balanced lifestyle* for *healthy minds and bodies*, focusing on coherent sentence and paragraph construction.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">3-5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>